    </region>
    <region left="20" top="1391" width="470" height="25" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 10px;">- spot check, G.c at the last point:</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="510" top="1391" width="1080" height="29" color="#000000" fontSize="8" isBreakable="false">
    <math decimalPlaces="4">
      <input>
        <e type="operand">g.2a</e>
        <e type="operand">G.c</e>
        <e type="operand">N.g</e>
        <e type="operand">2</e>
        <e type="function" args="3">el</e>
        <e type="operator" args="2">:</e>
      </input>
        <result action="numeric">
          <e type="operand">0.5096</e>
        </result>
    </math>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="1428" width="470" height="25" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 10px;">- and its f_n, must be 2.20:</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="510" top="1428" width="1080" height="29" color="#000000" fontSize="8" isBreakable="false">
    <math decimalPlaces="4">
      <input>
        <e type="operand">g.2b</e>
        <e type="operand">G.c</e>
        <e type="operand">N.g</e>
        <e type="operand">1</e>
        <e type="function" args="3">el</e>
        <e type="operator" args="2">:</e>
      </input>
        <result action="numeric">
          <e type="operand">2.2000</e>
        </result>
    </math>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="1481" width="1560" height="27" color="#0f2a44" fontSize="12" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 12px;">G2b  which grid division does this SMath use?   pick the frame that fits</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="1516" width="1560" height="51" color="#444444" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">All four frames below ask for the SAME window - f_n 0.50 to 2.25, gain 0 to 5 - and differ only in the pixels-per-division constant used to compute it. Exactly one of them should show the full-load curve filling the frame, rising to about 1.42 near f_n 0.69</p>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">and falling to about 0.51 at the right edge. The others will show it squeezed into a corner or not at all.</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="1575" width="1560" height="23" color="#444444" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">This block exists because the constant was wrong once and a comment in the code claimed it was right. A picture settles it; a comment does not.</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="1606" width="540" height="260" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
    <plot grid="true" axes="true" type="2d" render="lines" scale_x="0.064814815" scale_y="0.384615385" scale_z="1" rotate_x="0" rotate_y="0" rotate_z="0" transpose_x="-424" transpose_y="-130" transpose_z="0">
      <input>
        <e type="operand">G.c</e>
      </input>
    </plot>
    </region>
    <region left="580" top="1606" width="540" height="260" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
    <plot grid="true" axes="true" type="2d" render="lines" scale_x="0.025925926" scale_y="0.153846154" scale_z="1" rotate_x="0" rotate_y="0" rotate_z="0" transpose_x="-424" transpose_y="-130" transpose_z="0">
      <input>
        <e type="operand">G.c</e>
      </input>
    </plot>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="1898" width="540" height="260" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
    <plot grid="true" axes="true" type="2d" render="lines" scale_x="0.012962963" scale_y="0.076923077" scale_z="1" rotate_x="0" rotate_y="0" rotate_z="0" transpose_x="-424" transpose_y="-130" transpose_z="0">
      <input>
        <e type="operand">G.c</e>
      </input>
    </plot>
    </region>
    <region left="580" top="1898" width="540" height="260" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
    <plot grid="true" axes="true" type="2d" render="lines" scale_x="0.006481481" scale_y="0.038461538" scale_z="1" rotate_x="0" rotate_y="0" rotate_z="0" transpose_x="-424" transpose_y="-130" transpose_z="0">
      <input>
        <e type="operand">G.c</e>
      </input>
    </plot>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="2190" width="1560" height="51" color="#444444" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">- left to right, top row then bottom:  division = 20 px,  8 px,  4 px,  2 px. The sheet below is drawn with 4 px, which is what P19 of the probe sheet measured. If a different frame above is the right one, that number is what smsheet.Sheet.PLOT_DIV should</p>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">become.</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="2265" width="1560" height="27" color="#0f2a44" fontSize="12" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 12px;">G3  ONE curve in ONE frame        expected: full-load curve, peak about 1.42</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="2300" width="1560" height="23" color="#444444" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">x is f_n from 0.55 to 2.20, y is gain from 0 to 5, both fixed - SMath has no auto-scale, and a curve outside the window looks like an empty plot rather than an error. Mouse wheel zooms, dragging pans.</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="2331" width="540" height="300" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
    <plot grid="true" axes="true" type="2d" render="lines" scale_x="0.012962963" scale_y="0.066666667" scale_z="1" rotate_x="0" rotate_y="0" rotate_z="0" transpose_x="-424" transpose_y="-150" transpose_z="0">
      <input>
        <e type="operand">G.c</e>
      </input>
    </plot>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="2679" width="1560" height="27" color="#0f2a44" fontSize="12" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 12px;">G4  FOUR curves in FOUR frames                  expected: four separate curves</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="2714" width="1560" height="23" color="#444444" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">Q = 0.2, 0.4, 0.766, 1.5, left to right, top row then bottom. Lighter load gives the taller, more peaked curve. This is the fallback if G5 comes up empty, and it costs nothing but page area.</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="2745" width="540" height="300" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
    <plot grid="true" axes="true" type="2d" render="lines" scale_x="0.012962963" scale_y="0.066666667" scale_z="1" rotate_x="0" rotate_y="0" rotate_z="0" transpose_x="-424" transpose_y="-150" transpose_z="0">
      <input>
        <e type="operand">G.a</e>
      </input>
    </plot>
    </region>
    <region left="580" top="2745" width="540" height="300" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
    <plot grid="true" axes="true" type="2d" render="lines" scale_x="0.012962963" scale_y="0.066666667" scale_z="1" rotate_x="0" rotate_y="0" rotate_z="0" transpose_x="-424" transpose_y="-150" transpose_z="0">
      <input>
        <e type="operand">G.b</e>
      </input>
    </plot>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="3077" width="540" height="300" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
    <plot grid="true" axes="true" type="2d" render="lines" scale_x="0.012962963" scale_y="0.066666667" scale_z="1" rotate_x="0" rotate_y="0" rotate_z="0" transpose_x="-424" transpose_y="-150" transpose_z="0">
      <input>
        <e type="operand">G.c</e>
      </input>
    </plot>
    </region>
    <region left="580" top="3077" width="540" height="300" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
    <plot grid="true" axes="true" type="2d" render="lines" scale_x="0.012962963" scale_y="0.066666667" scale_z="1" rotate_x="0" rotate_y="0" rotate_z="0" transpose_x="-424" transpose_y="-150" transpose_z="0">
      <input>
        <e type="operand">G.d</e>
      </input>
    </plot>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="3425" width="1560" height="27" color="#0f2a44" fontSize="12" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 12px;">G5  FOUR curves in ONE frame                    THIS IS THE QUESTION</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="3460" width="1560" height="51" color="#444444" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">sys(G.a, G.b, G.c, G.d, 4, 1) stacks the four matrices into one plot input. The construct is taken from Project Admittance Copy.sm, which puts S1(x) and S2(x) in one frame the same way. If the frame below shows four curves the family plot is available; if</p>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">it is empty or red, use G4 instead. SMath prints no legend, so the curves would have to be named in text - lighter load is the taller curve.</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="3519" width="1100" height="380" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
    <plot grid="true" axes="true" type="2d" render="lines" scale_x="0.006363636" scale_y="0.052631579" scale_z="1" rotate_x="0" rotate_y="0" rotate_z="0" transpose_x="-864" transpose_y="-190" transpose_z="0">
      <input>
        <e type="operand">G.a</e>
        <e type="operand">G.b</e>
        <e type="operand">G.c</e>
        <e type="operand">G.d</e>
        <e type="operand">4</e>
        <e type="operand">1</e>
        <e type="function" preserve="true" args="6">sys</e>
      </input>
    </plot>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="3931" width="1560" height="23" color="#444444" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">What to report back: which of G3, G4, G5 drew, and whether any region is red.</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
  </regions>
</worksheet>
